 sensor stage vs OpenCV's: BIT-EXACT (0 pixels differ over 1709 frames)

--- CRITERION 4: speed against the byte-per-pixel denominator ---
 binCV :    1.048 ms/frame
 OpenCV :    3.780 ms/frame
 RATIO : 3.61x
 NOTE: binCV builds ONE pyramid per frame (the previous frame's is
 swapped in, proven bit-identical under BINCV_PYR_CHECK=1). OpenCV's
 calcOpticalFlowPyrLK rebuilds both of its pyramids per call; the
 redundancy was symmetric, and removing it on binCV's side only is the
 owner's recorded decision, not an oversight in the comparison.
 NOTE: OpenCV threads = 1 (binCV is single-threaded, always), and its
 LK and gftt are SIMD-vectorized.
 binCV has its AVX2 paths here -- the eight-keypoint LK batch,
 the sensor stage's median and edge kernels, and packing --
 so this is SIMD against SIMD, as the aarch64 arm is. What
 measured remains the point: at equal threads binCV LEADS, and the
 x86 deficit reported before it was THREADS, not vector width.
